course: biology
lesson: 141
title: Zootopia: real foxes and rabbits
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Zootopia, Nick Wilde the fox is a sly trickster and Judy Hopps the rabbit is a brave police officer, and the two become best friends.
explain: Real red foxes are not sly villains: they are careful, curious animals that learn fast and eat almost anything, which is why they live everywhere from the Arctic to the middle of London.
explain: Real rabbits are not brave officers either: they are shy, watchful prey that hear danger early with their big ears and bolt before a fox gets close.
explain: Picture a city garden at midnight: a fox trots past the bins and somewhere a rabbit freezes, then zigzags away. In real life that is a hunt, not a friendship, and both animals are only doing what keeps them alive.
fact: Red foxes are clever and adaptable; «sly» is a human story, and they eat mice, rabbits, worms, insects, fruit and scraps.
fact: Red foxes live on more continents than any other wild meat-eating mammal, and many live in cities such as London.
fact: City foxes are active mostly at night and find food in gardens and bins, so they need smaller areas than country foxes.
fact: A fox hunting in snow listens for mice underneath, leaps high and dives head first onto them; one unproven idea says foxes use the Earth's magnetism to aim.
fact: The scream heard on winter nights is often a fox calling to find a mate or mark its patch; foxes also bark, yelp and chatter, which is called gekkering.
fact: Fennec foxes of the Sahara have enormous ears full of blood vessels that give off heat, and the ears also hear prey moving underground.
fact: Foxes belong to the dog family but have vertical slit pupils like cats, which close tight in sun and open wide in the dark.
fact: Rabbits have excellent hearing; each long ear can turn on its own to catch sounds from any side, and the ears help them cool down.
fact: A mother rabbit can have several litters a year with about 4 to 8 babies each, and because so many rabbits are eaten by hunters, lots of babies keep their numbers up, so in Zootopia Judy's 275 brothers and sisters are a joke built on a real fact.
fact: Real rabbits are shy and freeze or run at the first sign of danger; they can sprint at about 50 km per hour and zigzag.
fact: In nature foxes hunt rabbits, and when rabbit numbers fall, fox numbers often fall a little later.
fact: Rabbits do eat crops and young vegetables, so farmers see them as pests and fence their fields.
exercise: Is a real red fox «sly» like Nick Wilde? | No, it is a careful, curious animal that learns fast; «sly» is a human story | Yes, foxes plan clever tricks to cheat other animals out of their dinner, just like Nick | Yes, foxes are the only animals that tell lies | No, foxes are slow and not very clever
exercise: Why can red foxes live in so many places? | They eat almost anything, from mice to fruit and scraps | They eat only rabbits, which live everywhere | They can go a whole year without food | They grow a thick new coat of fur to match each different place they move to
exercise: When is a city fox mostly out and about? | At night, when people are asleep | At midday, when the streets are busy | Only in summer, and never in winter | Only when it rains heavily and streets are empty
exercise: How does a fox catch a mouse under the snow? | It listens, leaps high and dives head first onto it | It digs a long tunnel from far away | It waits quietly beside the spot until the mouse comes out of the snow in spring | It smells the mouse and blows the snow away
exercise: What is the scream heard on a winter night often? | A fox calling to find a mate or mark its patch | A hedgehog that has been stepped on by someone walking home in the dark | A rabbit warning its family that a fox is nearby | A cat that has lost its home and wants to be let in
exercise: How do a fennec fox's enormous ears help it in the Sahara? | They give off heat and hear prey moving underground | They store water inside them to drink during the long dry season in the desert | They shade its eyes from the sand | They scare off lions in the desert
exercise: What shape are a fox's pupils? | Vertical slits, like a cat's | Round, like a person's | Square, like a goat's | Star-shaped, like an owl's
exercise: Which family does the fox belong to? | The dog family | The wild cat family | The weasel family | The bear family
exercise: How does a real rabbit hear danger from far away? | Each long ear turns on its own to catch sounds | It presses its ear to the ground | It listens with its nose | It can hear only sounds straight in front of it, so it turns its whole body round
exercise: Judy has 275 brothers and sisters. What real fact is that joke built on? | A mother rabbit can have several litters a year | Rabbits live for over a hundred years, so a family slowly grows very large | All rabbits in a field are brothers from one big nest | Rabbits lay hundreds of eggs at once in their burrows
exercise: What does a real rabbit do at the first sign of danger? | Freezes or runs and zigzags away | Stands up and fights the danger | Calls the other animals for help | Lies on its back and plays dead
exercise: A fox and a rabbit meet in a real field. What usually happens? | The fox tries to catch and eat the rabbit | They play together like friends | The rabbit chases the fox away | They ignore each other completely
exercise: Do real rabbits eat farm crops like Judy's family grows? | Yes, so farmers see them as pests and fence fields | No, rabbits eat only wild grass and never touch a crop that a farmer has planted | No, rabbits eat only carrots that are cooked | Yes, but only the roots, never the leaves
exercise: Why do so many rabbits get born each year? | Many are eaten by hunters, so lots of babies keep numbers up | Rabbits live so long they need company | Rabbits need many helpers to dig burrows | Farmers feed them so well with spare carrots and lettuce that they breed fast
exercise: How fast can a rabbit sprint for a short burst? | About 50 km per hour | Only about 5 km per hour | About 150 km per hour | About 500 km per hour
exercise: What happens to fox numbers when rabbit numbers fall? | Fox numbers often fall a little later | Fox numbers rise straight away | Fox numbers never change at all | Foxes start to eat grass instead
test: «2 foxes and 3 rabbits in the garden last night, how many ears altogether» | 10 | 5 | 8 | 12
test: «Nick's a fox so he's basically a cat, right? look at his eyes» What is right? | No, foxes are in the dog family; they just share slit pupils with cats | Yes, foxes are a kind of wild cat | Yes, foxes and cats are really the same animal, which is why their eyes look alike | No, foxes are in the bear family
test: In a cartoon, a rabbit stands its ground and punches a fox on the nose. What is real? | A real rabbit would freeze or bolt and zigzag away | Real rabbits often stand up and box foxes on the nose until the fox runs off | Real rabbits are stronger than foxes and kick them away | Real foxes are scared of rabbits and keep well away
test: «bloody fox screaming outside again at 2am, is someone being murdered» What is it most likely? | A fox calling, which is normal on winter nights | A fox that is badly hurt every time | A rabbit screaming for help | An owl that has caught a cat
test: What would happen if a fennec fox had small ears like a red fox? | It would struggle to lose heat and hear prey under the sand | It would hear better in the desert | It would stay cooler in the sun | Nothing at all would change for it, because ears do nothing to keep an animal cool
test: «mate says urban foxes are lost country foxes that can't get home» What is right? | No, many foxes live in cities for good, with food and few enemies | Yes, they are all lost country foxes that wandered in and keep trying to get home | Yes, they come in only for one night | No, foxes in cities are a different kind of dog
test: «4 foxes on the camera, 1 of them gone off, how many fox legs still on screen» | 12 | 16 | 4 | 20
test: In Zootopia carrots are Judy's family crop. Why do real farmers fence fields against rabbits? | Rabbits nibble soft young crops and can ruin them | Rabbits dig up the fences for fun | Rabbits steal tools from the farm | Rabbits are scared of fences, so the farmer only has to put one up and they leave
test: A fox in snow faces a spot, then leaps and dives head first. What is it using to find the mouse? | Its sharp hearing of sounds under the snow | Its eyes, which see through snow | Its tail, which feels the ground | Its nose only, with ears folded back
test: «Judy's a rabbit cop, so rabbits must be brave, innit» What is right? | Real rabbits are shy prey that run from danger, which keeps them alive | Yes, rabbits chase off foxes and cats | Yes, real rabbits stay awake and guard the other animals of the field at night | No, rabbits are not real animals at all
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the character attributed to the red fox in popular narrative. | The epithet «sly» is a human invention; the animal is cautious, curious and quick to learn | The red fox is demonstrably deceitful, devising tricks to cheat other species of their food | The red fox is incapable of learning from experience | The red fox subsists exclusively upon rabbits
exam: Identify the principal function of the exceptionally large ears of the fennec fox. | The dissipation of bodily heat and the detection of prey beneath the sand | The storage of water during periods of drought | The shading of the eyes from solar glare | The intimidation of larger predators, such as lions, by a sudden display of the ears
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the red fox and the rabbit in nature? | The fox is a predator of the rabbit, and fox numbers tend to follow rabbit numbers | The two species customarily form lasting friendships and share a burrow through the winter | The rabbit is a predator of the fox | The two species never encounter one another
exam: Ascertain the response of a wild rabbit upon first detecting danger. | It remains motionless or flees at speed in a zigzag course | It advances upon the source of danger | It summons other species to its assistance by means of loud and repeated cries | It feigns death by lying upon its back
